{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on question f0bf343d, identifiers (c+d): option A. The room\narchive keeps its Rules replay root by expected role, and it reads no\nRules birth for the selection.\n\nWhy A. Ruling 429c1be7 says that a kind lookup follows a read. Room\nmode reads no Rules birth today, by design, and the kind in the old\nreference was the sender's label, not a proof. B adds a paid read to\nevery lane pass, or a new unavailable result, to replace a label.\n\nThe construction, as your question states it.\n\n1. One archive-only expected-role record, for the Rules slot of the\n   checked Directory row. It comes only from the bounded Directory\n   query and the checks of archive-target.ts steps 1 to 3. Nothing\n   else makes one: no digest, no manifest row, no caller label, no\n   private caller record of ruling 70186a25.\n2. The record is selection metadata. It never becomes a\n   CheckedSequence, a verified birth, `from.pin` evidence or replayed\n   provenance, and it skips no check of the pin, the cohort, the\n   creator, the repository, the archive privacy or Replay.\n3. When an existing read of the pass reaches the Rules genesis, the\n   reader checks the full sequence association and the kind `rules`\n   before it uses the bytes. A read that contradicts the role stops\n   the pass by its existing failure path. It supplies no proof and\n   publishes no byte of that source.\n4. Every other replay root needs a CheckedSequence from bytes that the\n   pass already reads. A bare extra root with neither a checked\n   sequence nor the Rules record is no root: the pass stops through\n   its existing unavailable path. No fallback read follows.\n5. The guards stay: 64 roots, full references, no alias, the local\n   root, and no Register, Membership or Inbox root. `Mark.replayScopes`\n   stays exact selection metadata. A stored row recovers no kind, and\n   a rebuild compares the exact ordered closure with the stored Mark.\n6. No Native RPC, no source open, no cache and no deadline is added.\n\nOne observation for the core lead, to use only if the source agrees. A\nscope ID is the digest of its seed, and the seed holds the kind. So\nwhere a pass already holds the seed of a child, as in the genesis of\nits Directory, `scopeIdOf(seed)` equal to the reference's scope proves\nthe kind of that address for every sequence under it. That is more\nthan a role and less than a CheckedSequence, because it says nothing\nabout which genesis exists. If the Rules slot check already compares\nthat digest, say so in the note and name the record by what it proves.\n\nThe witnesses are the ones you list. OPS-2 keeps archive-build and\narchive-publication. Assign the other four files in the (c+d) plan.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}